==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*deuzą}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*dʰewsóm}}.

Germanic cognates include {{cog|osx|dior}}, {{cog|nl|dier}}, {{cog|goh|tior}} ({{cog|de|Tier}}), {{cog|non|dýr}} ({{cog|da|dyr}}, {{cog|sv|djur}}), {{cog|got|𐌳𐌹𐌿𐍃}}. The Indo-European root also led to {{cog|lt|dvēsti}}, {{cog|lv|dvēsele}}, {{cog|sla-pro|*duša}} ({{cog|ru|душа}}).

